WEBVTT

00:00:02.197 --> 00:00:18.734
All right. So my name is Marcus and I welcome you to the Jung Helpdesk meetup group and a very special event with the topic of life. This is the biggest meetup group in Europe, which is not very hard because there's only one group.

00:00:20.226 --> 00:00:40.357
And I start this meetup group this year, but I played around longer with the thought because I wanted to give a platform where it's possible to get to know Jungian concepts in a very, let's say, pure manner. I read a lot of Jung.

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:56.897
I read almost all the books that are commercially available, and it took me now almost 3 years where I read every day 20 pages. And he has written really a lot there. You have the whole Collected Works, there's 19 books. It's just like a meter of books, fat stack.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:17.376
And you have seminars, you have his autobiography, you have talks of him, he has letters, a lot of books with letters. And I always tried to find videos or podcasts or articles describing Jungian concepts to understand them better.

00:01:17.424 --> 00:01:35.834
And the more I read about those, the more I realized that they're all a little bit mushy and they're missing some aspects because Jung has really, really a lot of aspects and a lot of them get dropped. So I realized the concepts are not so good and I explained how they explained were not so good.

00:01:36.445 --> 00:01:53.460
And I joined a lot of other meetups and people were there always very open and very curious about Jungian concepts. And I thought, okay, good, I start my own group and We have almost 180 people, which is great. Events are every 2 weeks.

00:01:54.638 --> 00:02:10.525
And normally the structure for the new guys is I give a little presentation about a topic and then we can get in a discussion about it to go more in depth and also get your viewpoint on it.

00:02:11.625 --> 00:02:35.397
And yeah, so I would talk a little bit about Jung's life first, because it fits very nicely to this today's topic. So Jung was born in Switzerland, 1875, and his father was a pastor and his mother was more like an ordinary woman.

00:02:36.134 --> 00:02:52.506
And they had this, let's say, inside joke that he maybe took a little bit too serious that he might be the unofficial grandson of Goethe, and this spawned a lifelong interest of him into Goethe and his works.

00:02:53.693 --> 00:03:11.771
And he grew up in a bit of a rural area that he didn't leave really until his 18th birthday. And he moved after school to Basel to learn there as a student, and there he came into contact with psychology and said, this is really what I want to do.

00:03:12.541 --> 00:03:22.260
And first he didn't want to do it because his father was a little bit psychologically inclined and had a little bit of training there, but he did it in secret.

00:03:22.324 --> 00:03:42.715
So he studied, finished in this very new field of psychotherapy and psychology, which was back then cutting-edge science, how to deal with people who have psychological problems, and it was really a big shift in time of the whole mentality, how do we treat people who have psychological problems?

00:03:42.796 --> 00:03:57.150
Because back then, the consensus was that people have damaged brains, and basically you can't help them. You just put them away in some hole and wait till they die. So the conditions were horrendous. The treatment was horrendous.

00:03:57.953 --> 00:04:12.448
And this changed with the emergence of psychoanalysis and with Freud, who proposed this talking therapy to help people work through their problems through words.

00:04:14.027 --> 00:04:36.137
After university, he started to practice in an institution called Burghölzli, who dealt with schizophrenics and neuroses and, and so on. And there he started really academic career. He started also to teach. He made experiments. Read papers, published papers, got pretty famous already in those circles.

00:04:36.249 --> 00:04:58.687
And there he came into contact with Freud's ideas and he, uh, was an instant defender of Freud. And they met then later, and the first time they met, they talked for 13 hours straight. And out of this became a very, very close friendship. And Freud was already older than Jung, roughly by 20 years, or I think even more.

00:04:59.103 --> 00:05:13.469
So Freud was looking for a successor for to bring psychoanalysis in a new era and to defend what already has been established. Because there was always a lot of pressure on psychology and psychoanalysis.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:34.528
And this friendship lasted 6 years, but they diverged at the end because Carl Gustav Jung was always fascinated with the occult, the spiritual. Religion and so on. And Freud wasn't really a big fan of it. And he thought that Jung might grow out of it.

00:05:35.106 --> 00:05:53.088
And he even thought that Jung had a father complex that was just fostering his interest in religion and so on. For which Jung replied, no, I don't have a father complex. I have a mother complex, which is maybe the most psychoanalytic rebuttal you can think of.

00:05:53.954 --> 00:06:14.172
And Once they departed, which was about 1913, Jung started to have visions, pretty strong visions that weren't known to the public until much later. And in these visions, he saw Europe drowning in blood and corpses, and a voice telling him that all this will come to pass.

00:06:15.113 --> 00:06:35.802
And he thought he was going crazy up until the point when the First World War started, 1914, where he got a little bit relieved. Because this was like a classic prophetic vision. And he started to record his dreams and use a technique called active imagination where you try to get into your fantasy world.

00:06:36.746 --> 00:06:52.212
And he started to write down everything and he collected it in Black Books. And out of that, he created The Red Book, which is a distillation of these experiences and to find like a common theme, common threads, and so on.

00:06:54.377 --> 00:07:08.231
And with these visions and with this break of Freud, his academic career died because Freud was incredibly important for psychology and psychoanalysis, and he pretty much sealed the fate, the academic fate of Jung.

00:07:08.716 --> 00:07:29.494
So he stopped teaching and he only practiced and helping patients, like a little practice and therapeutic office. And this is where a new stage of Jung's life started. And it was, I think, roughly with 38. And just remember this number, it will come up again.

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:55.330
And in this investigation into his inner world, he laid the foundations for everything that Jung is mostly known for: the collective unconscious, anima, animus, shadow. And these cultural aspects became very important for him. So in the past, it was extremely academic, extremely scientific, psychoanalysis, publishing papers, giving talks, and so on.

00:07:56.376 --> 00:08:11.001
And here, this spiritual part of his life takes over. And he does this for 13 years, and he tries to explore scientifically the experiences he had and builds all those very interesting concepts about how the psyche is structured.

00:08:11.931 --> 00:08:35.379
And he draws from different sources of mythology to find common themes and threads, to get insight into human nature, basically the part in us that is, that we have common with everybody else and every other person in the world. And he does this, as I said, for 13 years until he gets a copy in his hand.

00:08:35.427 --> 00:08:57.564
It's a translated book about Chinese alchemy. The Secret of the Golden Flower, and he is asked to write the foreword for it. And he reads the translation and finds extreme overlap between his experience and this experience. These, let's say, the wisdom formulated in the book.

00:08:58.546 --> 00:09:19.578
And this is a very old book, a very old Chinese book. And this is when he really starts to investigate a lot of alchemy, and he won't stop till the end of his life talking about alchemy. And he sees an unique chance in alchemy to understand not only how the mind works, but also the, the modern mind.

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:45.999
So extremely prolific, works till the end of his life, which was 1961. So born in 1875, died in 1961. And shortly before his death, he wrote a little book called Man and His Symbols. And this is an introductory work into his concepts and ideas and theories, his whole body of work.

00:09:46.662 --> 00:10:02.774
And he's written really, as I said, a lot, very complex and a lot of different facets. And what I've noticed when I encountered other explanations about Jung, that they're often missing this psychoanalytic part.

00:10:02.937 --> 00:10:22.633
So he, diverged from psychoanalysis with his departure, this separation from Freud, and he founded something of a movement called analytical psychology, which is different than psychoanalysis, but a lot of foundations are the same. So it's a little bit thinking about libido differently and so on.

00:10:22.697 --> 00:10:35.030
But Jung's work is on a very firm psychoanalytic foundation. All the work later in life, it's always built on the former phase of his life.

00:10:35.623 --> 00:10:59.997
So this is why the alchemy stuff is so hard to understand, because you have to understand everything that Jung has written before to get an idea why he's so interested in this very, very niche topic. So this would be the story about his life. And now I want to tell you about his views on life in general.

00:11:01.316 --> 00:11:14.766
So, like a normal basic life that everybody goes through. So, for those who read the description for this event, I had a very specific metaphor in there, and it's from Jung.

00:11:15.394 --> 00:11:36.309
It's that of the sun during the day, so that it rises from the horizon, travels over the sky, and then disappears again behind the horizon. And Jung describes the life of people in this way, so especially the consciousness of people.

00:11:36.759 --> 00:12:01.065
So when a human being is born and it's a child, it's this first rays of sunlight coming from the horizon, the sunrise. When you have little children, they have close to no real— he calls it ego, the center of consciousness.

00:12:02.077 --> 00:12:26.757
This is an area which, or say that's a phenomena more, that is able to experience and to assert will. This is lacking in children. Like children are pure instincts and they are pure human nature. And it takes a while for this ego to emerge out of this, let's say, black mass of unconsciousness.

00:12:26.967 --> 00:12:49.316
It's like this little island coming out of the sea. This is also a metaphor that Carl Gustav Jung uses very often. And so in the beginning, consciousness is very fragile, very easy to disturb. When you have a child, it has difficulty to do anything willfully for long stretches of time, but this gets better when the child gets older.

00:12:50.060 --> 00:13:11.428
So the world of the child is extremely subjective. So everybody who had contact with smaller children knows that they make up the rules of a game newly while playing the game. So you try to get something objective to them. It's like, this is a game and these are the rules.

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:25.987
And when the child is very young, incredibly hard to play the game because the child will say, no, I'm winning because of that. And no, this doesn't happen and so on. So it's pure subjectivity and you can even do tests on it.

00:13:26.035 --> 00:13:48.083
So in Germany, for those who are not grown up in Germany, before a child can enter school, elementary school, they have to do a test and it's like a little bit physical test, but also a cognitive test where they get certain tasks and they just look if the child is able to do it. And one task, for example, is what the name of your parents.

00:13:49.230 --> 00:14:11.926
To really dissociate mother and father, that they also have a name, which is different than mother and father. And also like, okay, draw, for example, a human being and whether the child really does this and drawn a human being instead draws a dinosaur. I know this because I drawn the dinosaur. Which almost got me not enrolled into school.

00:14:13.266 --> 00:14:37.095
It's, it's just that this possibility and ability to steer actions and to adhere to objective factors, to believe that things are happening outside of yourself, that the world is more than yourself, is an important part of developmental psychology. When this is not yet developed, Children are incredibly close to the collective unconscious.

00:14:37.256 --> 00:14:53.107
So they're very open for mythology and fairy tales and so on, because these are products of the collective unconscious. And the dreams that children have— Jung was also very dream— big into dream interpretation. That's because of his psychoanalytic roots.

00:14:54.471 --> 00:15:13.396
Uh, archetypal in nature, you get images and pictures and situations which are not from the subjective situation of the child, but rather of collective source where you have images that popped up in different cultures, different epochs that were completely separated from each other.

00:15:15.728 --> 00:15:36.933
And Jung noted that this could be one of the reasons why in the eastern part of the world, children were seen as born again because they had this weird specific knowledge about specific cultural concepts. Where people thought, okay, they already know this is because they are born again.

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:57.467
So children are still completely in the subjective. They're still this little sun rising over the horizon. And it is the scope of the world gets bigger and bigger as the sun rises and as the consciousness develops and as the person gets older. And it's first this absolute subjectivity.

00:15:58.751 --> 00:16:19.844
Everything exists because of me, that expands to the family, to immediate mother, father, mother, father become the whole world. The family becomes the whole world. And then it starts just seeping in, ah, my immediate surroundings are the whole world. And the world gets bigger and bigger and bigger as a child progresses.

00:16:21.401 --> 00:16:39.510
And This is the trajectory of a person, of consciousness, that it rises and rises and expanding and expanding. This cultivation of consciousness and of will is one of the main goals for education.

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:56.886
Like when you educate a small child in school or at home, you try to get them to be able to do complex things for a longer amount of time, to do and act in willful actions and so on, to follow rules.

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:14.165
And this pushes the child more and more away from the family to the society at large, to establish itself there, to not only get an education but get a job.

00:17:14.715 --> 00:17:30.403
Get a career, find a partner, start a family, have a house, have hobbies, get recognition, to do things that are socially positively regarded and to establish themselves in the world.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:53.035
And when something fails, there's always a new chance, there's always new time, there's always a hopeful outlook into the future because it's establishing, it's ever-expanding, it's everyday new possibilities. And Jung says this is right up to the point where the sun is at the highest point.

00:17:54.435 --> 00:18:08.449
And then this shift takes place. So, as I said, when you have a very small child, it's extremely formed by the collective unconscious. Basically, it's just living the collective unconscious and it's living out instincts.

00:18:09.109 --> 00:18:21.628
And what the education does and the first half of life does is establishing consciousness to be able to do many things, understand many things, know many things, have experienced many things, and to act willfully.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:45.119
And at this midpoint where the sun starts slowly its descent again, this is when the collective gets back again. And it's the unconscious that's pushing up this, okay, life is more than your individual achievements in society.

00:18:46.523 --> 00:18:55.640
This midpoint of life that Jung describes is around 35 to 40. I think it's his age. The age he mentions the most is 38.

00:18:56.624 --> 00:19:20.962
So if you remember Jung's life, This was also at his point, 38, where he had suddenly these visions and his academic life came to an end and he was firmly established at the world. And one thing I haven't told you, he married the second richest woman in Switzerland at this point, which was a very good idea.

00:19:21.027 --> 00:19:45.319
So when you have the chance, marry the second richest. Person of any country, and he had many children with her, and they were living in Küsnacht, very nice villa right next to a lake. He had his own sailboat, and every time he had to think, he would get into his sailboat. But at this midpoint of life, he describes that people notice that they're dissatisfied suddenly.

00:19:45.447 --> 00:20:05.907
They have achieved everything they wanted. They have established themselves. They had a career. And for some reason, they're not happy anymore. Today we would call it midlife crisis, but back then this term did not exist yet. And all those old solutions are suddenly not working anymore because the person is not a young person anymore.

00:20:06.469 --> 00:20:22.574
The shift from expanding, expanding, expanding now reverses and it's getting less and less and less. He says with the movement past the midpoint of life, the psyche is developing for death. It's anticipating death.

00:20:23.505 --> 00:20:45.834
So this is a big fear of the second part of life, the, to have less chances, to have less possibilities, and also that all the values and all the solutions you had before are suddenly not working anymore because the psychological needs are different. This is where the collective comes up, and this is where the figure of the anima comes up.

00:20:48.785 --> 00:21:10.342
And Jung said, especially in modern culture, modern Western culture, that we don't have really an education or an institution to catch people at this point of life, to help them in the second part. He really described it as What you do in the first part of life, this is all field study.

00:21:11.338 --> 00:21:34.218
It's collective in the sense as societal, as you try to fit, conform society and achieve society goals. And the second part of life is that you start to engage in cultural creative endeavors. That is, this is where culture is coming from. There's also another example from Nietzsche.

00:21:35.166 --> 00:21:52.812
Nietzsche, who also was a professor in Basel, same university where Jung studied, not at the same time, Nietzsche lived before. He wrote his book Thus Spoke Zarathustra at the age of 38 and 10 days. And he spent the rest of his life trying to understand what he's written.

00:21:53.807 --> 00:22:17.174
And it's this emergence of impersonal collective ideas that suddenly emerge that really dominate the second part of life. And there we don't really have any cultural institutional support structures. Carl Gustav Jung always talked about, he wished there would be schools for adults.

00:22:17.416 --> 00:22:36.908
He said it's a, it's a huge tragedy that there's only a school to get people to the point where they can get a job and get married. And buy a house, for example, but that there's nothing beyond that and that the psychological needs change. And that this is the point where depressions, for example, set in at this midpoint of life because suddenly people realize they're not happy.

00:22:37.621 --> 00:22:54.699
And he noticed this, he noticed this through his psychoanalytic work as he found out that Freud is always usable in the first half of life when you have to get people out of the basement of their parents.

00:22:54.860 --> 00:23:18.221
Like, okay, move past your father, move past your mother, try to find the father in the job and in the workplace where you will go, try to find the mother in a partner, but really to get people out into the world. And this is the goal of Freudian psychology, to get these people out of the infantile stage of their life.

00:23:18.881 --> 00:23:42.589
So he realized that all these techniques from Freud and also Adler, who said the same things as Freud but had not sex as a basis, but power, they did not, they could not work for people over this midpoint, people older than 35 or 40. And the problem was you can't really lure them outside because they are outside.

00:23:43.050 --> 00:24:03.768
They have established themselves. They have achieved what they wanted to achieve. So, but they're still unhappy. And this is a point where he would take them and let them, for example, draw to realize that something is inside them that is not personal, which is not consciousness, but something impersonal unconscious that's acting on its own and autonomously.

00:24:07.232 --> 00:24:32.364
So his field of study and his therapy was based pretty much on this, to get people closer to this collective ideas and these collective systems of the past that were a little bit better fitted to explain what is happening, because it's a fearful state to suddenly realize you're not the lord in your own house and there are things that are strange and incredible.

00:24:32.444 --> 00:24:56.951
This is also a point where he says that this midpoint of life where these strange archetypal dreams come up with strange imageries and strange situations. So the problem, as you might already realize, is the unconscious does something and the consciousness is lagging behind.

00:24:57.405 --> 00:25:18.249
It's a little bit like when you look into the sky and you hear a plane. When you try to look where the plane is, you won't find it. You have to see somewhere else because this disconnect between seeing the plane and hearing the plane. Is because the sound waves have to travel down to you and then you look up, but you don't hear where the plane is, but rather where it was.

00:25:18.922 --> 00:25:36.865
And it's a little bit like the relationship with unconscious and consciousness. The consciousness is always lagging behind. It's always the sound and the unconscious is a plane. It's the main driver. And it's this being behind the unconscious and keeping up with it in its movement.

00:25:37.633 --> 00:25:56.310
This is a difficult part because it's suddenly at the midpoint, it starts to shift and has a different focus. And the unconscious pushes the person to do things. This pushing out of the parents' basement, the unconscious also does.

00:25:57.145 --> 00:26:21.294
But when the conscious is not willing to do it, there's a friction and there's conflict in the psyche of the person. Which means complexes will be formed, neuroses will be formed, hysteria could emerge. It's this maladaptiveness to life that's causing people to seek out therapy in a psychoanalytic context.

00:26:22.464 --> 00:26:42.722
And the same happens, of course, in the second part of life, but you have all the impersonal stuff. So we're in the second part of life. And it is this compensating function of the unconscious that's always catching up to the consciousness. The consciousness is always limited. It's expanding over time, but it would like to remain the same.

00:26:43.075 --> 00:27:06.449
And it's always unconscious pushing against it and trying to get it into new directions. It's a huge compensation. And the compensation gets bigger and bigger the more you move away because the conscious is very adapted to look at one way of viewing the world and experiencing the world, that there are all these other ways to experience the world.

00:27:07.140 --> 00:27:28.353
You have your consciousness and when you concentrate on something, you blur out all the other things. And there's a very great video you can watch on YouTube. Okay, for those who don't know it, I will spoil it. But you see a group of basketball players and they have a basketball and they pass the ball. Between each other. And it's experiment.

00:27:28.819 --> 00:27:44.778
Normally the experimenter would say to the participant of the experiment, okay, try to count how many times the ball is passed. And it goes for 5, 10 minutes. And the person sits there and counts the balls. And then the experimenter comes back in and says, okay, how many times?

00:27:45.132 --> 00:27:55.689
And the person says something, it's not important because the experimenter will then say, okay, did you see the gorilla? And the person will say, what gorilla? Like 80% of the people, they don't see it.

00:27:56.827 --> 00:28:12.777
And they will rewind the tape and show that in the middle of the video, a guy in a huge gorilla costume goes right in the middle of the field of view of the video, bangs his chest, and moves to the other side. And people don't see it. That's consciousness.

00:28:13.034 --> 00:28:29.648
You're completely focused on one part of reality and you can keep it up You can become so proficient that you forget of all the other parts. And this is what the unconscious starts reminding you of the other parts. It's basically, I don't want to say the gorilla.

00:28:29.746 --> 00:28:50.469
No, let's say the gorilla at a certain point comes out of the video and starts banging against your head to remind you that it's also there. That's the unconscious for you. And how this is lived, I already alluded before, like when you're in the first part of life, people don't want to move out of the basement.

00:28:51.782 --> 00:29:04.009
In the second part of life, they don't want to get back in the house because they want to stay outside. They want to be young. They want to have the fast car. They want to have all the possibilities, even though that it's just not what the psyche needs anymore.

00:29:04.057 --> 00:29:26.916
So when you know the concept of midlife crisis, it's a really great description of what's happening psychologically there. And it is this holding on to the past and not able to move forward that is causing trouble in the psyche. So I'm in the basement, I don't want to get out.

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:48.619
So you come constantly arrested by the unconscious. Ah, I'm out, I'm established, but I want to keep expanding. At a certain point, the psyche will say, no, turn inwards, live in your internal reality. A little bit like Jung experienced that he started actively to engage with his unconscious and to record this and to create culture, to create art.

00:29:50.330 --> 00:30:05.223
The Red Book that was done by him is an incredible piece of art. He also draws, so it was a really huge, thick book, handwritten, hand-drawn. It took him many, many years. To create it. And it was hidden away.

00:30:05.947 --> 00:30:29.405
It wasn't known till 2011, I think, when it was published after, yeah, 50 years after his death, because he was concerned that people think he's crazy, but because it sounds a little bit crazy. So life is this always changing up and down movement. There's always conflict.

00:30:29.469 --> 00:30:50.119
There's always You know it when you try to get something, let's say you've tried to find a partner, you try to get a career, you try to buy a house or an apartment in Berlin, which is a crazy idea. Why would you do that? It's always, let's say, pain and suffering, but this means you're alive and you're doing the right thing. So there's no way to get out of it.

00:30:50.890 --> 00:31:11.019
The Buddha said it right, life is suffering, but at least you can choose your suffering. This is the sign that you're alive and that you're changing and evolving. Yeah. And at the end of life, there's death. And Jung says the second part of life is a preparation of death and the event that everything then normally should work towards.

00:31:14.598 --> 00:31:34.103
It's, which is a little bit of a weird concept. So this would be practically my little introduction into what Jung understands of life and his concept regarding life. And you already find a lot of other concepts very closely associated with this.

00:31:35.530 --> 00:32:00.304
Things like the unconscious, of course, the collective unconscious, consciousness, ego, anima, dreams, and this whole path that I try to explain is also very closely associated with individuation. So the way of becoming an individual. The seed of the tree becomes the tree. You already have the tree and the seed in the beginning, but it takes time to make it flourish.

00:32:00.385 --> 00:32:16.389
So the life of a person ideally is this flourishing of the psychological factors and to live a life that is specific to one's own, to one's own psychological needs.

00:32:18.399 --> 00:32:40.276
Yeah, this would be my presentation for this topic, and I hope this was understandable and a little bit entertaining. And talking about these things, do you have any feelings or thoughts about it, the way that Carl Gustav Jung talked about life?

